A||||9||GT Bastard||Which car to autoX????||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||01-07-2000||11:09 PM||||I'm getting into auto-xing this coming summer, and I want to prepare my car for it. The question is, which car should I auto-x.  I'm planning on putting some money into the suspension characteristics, and it's not practical for me to spend a lot of money on both cars for this.  I've got a '94 Z28 and an '87 Fiero GT.  Both cars handle great in stock form, but I've installed premium monroe shocks and struts in the the fiero fairly recently.  The camaro is mostly stock, but it's got new tires?  I know you guys are biased because you're Fiero-folk, but what do you think?  Both cars can be seen at <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/carbonatedjeff" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/carbonatedjeff</A> <P>thanks for the input guys||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||01-07-2000||11:24 PM||||I will probably get flamed from Camaro fans for this, but: If you are concerned about your safety, race the Fiero. I have watched the 4th gen. Camaros show up in salvage yards since that body was introduced. The entire door structure is plastic with a steel bar rivited to the plastic. I have seen these cars hit in the rear and both doors fall off of them! Even if you have a hard top, the roof under the plastic cover is still not a solid metal roof. I saw one where it looks like the passenger was ejected through the roof of the car through the "hole" where the t-top would be if the car would have had them. A local high school boy lost control of his camaro and side swiped a tree. The steel bar in the door came loose and was the cause of the major trauma that cost the young man his life. They are beautiful cars, but I have seen too many of them fall apart as a result of relatively minor accidents.<p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 01-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||01-07-2000||11:42 PM||||That's strange.. I've never heard of that before.  The car seems very solid and I've heard they hold up well in accidents, but ok. What sort of work you you do?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||01-07-2000||11:57 PM||||AutoX is class against class, but the Fiero has so much more potential that on a pure enjoyment level it would be the best pick by far.  1.00 lateral gee is not that much money for a Fiero to achieve, thats where I find my adrenalin.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||01-08-2000||12:03 AM||||I rebuild wrecked cars. In the past 15 years, I have only worked on 2 kinds of vehicles I would be afraid to own. The late model F body is one of them. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||01-08-2000||12:07 AM||||Lemme guess... The other is the Ford Pinto?? hehe...  Whoa, this has gone off the topic. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||01-08-2000||09:37 AM||||Bastard, you said you were just getting into auto-X racing. I'd think it would be easier to start out in the Fiero. Racing the Camaro will put you up in a higher class where you will be competing with lots of big boys. The Fieros do very well in their classes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||01-08-2000||01:01 PM||||What types of cars are in the same class as the Fiero?  I'm just curious what I would be up against.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||01-08-2000||04:46 PM||||It depends on the mods done to your vehicle to which class you will be in, in SCCA anyway. I raced in Street tire last year (any car with DOT tires is in this class. Any size tire and wheel can be used as long as you don't have to modify the body or suspension to mount them.) This class is divded up into several other classes. This gives a Yugo a chance to win against a Vette. <P>The V6 Fiero is in C stock and the F body is in F stock(I think). If you drive the Fiero you will be against: MR2 non-turbo 85-93, Porsche 944, RX-7 non-turbo, 240, 260, 280 Zs to name a few.<P>Go here and link around til you find what you are looking for  <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||01-08-2000||10:56 PM||||LOL @ GTB- no the other one is the mid size Jeep Cherokee- not the newer style, but like the boxey looking mid 80's on up. We fixed a roll over and the top was smashed down into the seats. In most other SUV's, the door and window posts have an outer sheet metal skin, an inner skin and a really thick layer of steel reinforcement between them. The jeeps just have the inner and outer skin without the extra support structure. While drilling out the spot welds to cut off the damaged roof, only about half the welds actually held up to the accident. ||||reg||1
